        "custom_liquid": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n  \"@graph\": [\n\t{\n  \t\"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n  \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/rhode-island-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#article\",\n  \t\"isPartOf\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/rhode-island-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n    \t\"@id\": \"https://strobesnmore.com/blogs/informative/rhode-island-emergency-vehicle-light-laws#webpage\"\n  \t},\n  \t\"headline\": \"Rhode Island Emergency Vehicle Light Laws: Colors, Permits and Vehicle Classes\",\n  \t\"description\": \"What R.I. Gen. Laws 31-24-31 allows by color and vehicle class, who needs a flashing lights permit, the $25 fee, and the 500 ft rule. Reviewed August 2026.\",\n  \t\"abstract\": \"Rhode Island prohibits flashing lights by default under R.I. Gen. Laws § 31-24-31(a). Every legal use is a listed exception, not a general allowance. Blue is reserved for state and local police units in Rhode Island under § 31-24-31(b)(3). No volunteer, tow, security or private vehicle class may display it. The privately owned vehicle of an authorized volunteer fire, rescue or ambulance member in Rhode Island may display red, white, and/or alternating flashing white, the same color set as department apparatus, under § 31-24-31(b)(1). Tow trucks, wreckers, utility trucks, snowplows and municipal maintenance vehicles in Rhode Island use amber. The statute provides for a $25 flashing lights permit fee for that category, waived for state, town and fire district vehicles. Rhode Island sets a 500 foot visibility standard and requires paired front and rear lamps to flash simultaneously rather than alternately.\",\n  \t\"speakable\": {\n    \t\"@type\": \"SpeakableSpecification\",\n    \t\"cssSelector\": [\n      \t\".key-takeaways\",\n      \t\"h1\"\n    \t]\n  \t},\n  \t\"inLanguage\": \"en-US\",\n  \t\"datePublish